---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import FaqBlock from '../../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../site';

const rows = [
  { dimension: 'What it is', ioss: 'Import One-Stop Shop — a single EU VAT registration for sub-€150 e-commerce imports', deferral: 'A licence to delay import VAT until goods enter a member state' },
  { dimension: 'Who it is for', ioss: 'B2C e-commerce sellers shipping small parcels', deferral: 'B2B importers holding inventory or re-exporting' },
  { dimension: 'Threshold', ioss: 'Consignments up to €150', deferral: 'No value threshold — applies to full containers' },
  { dimension: 'Main benefit', ioss: 'Buyer sees the full price; simplified clearance', deferral: 'Import VAT stays out of your cash flow' },
  { dimension: 'Where it lives', ioss: 'Any EU member state', deferral: 'Notably the Netherlands (Article 23)' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Are IOSS and VAT deferral the same thing?',
    a: 'No. IOSS is for B2C e-commerce parcels up to €150 — it simplifies VAT collection at the point of sale. VAT deferral (such as the Netherlands\u2019 Article 23) is for importers holding inventory or re-exporting — it delays import VAT until goods enter a member state. They solve different problems for different businesses.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should I use IOSS?',
    a: 'When you sell to EU consumers and ship consignments valued at €150 or less. You register once in a single member state, collect the buyer\u2019s VAT at checkout, and remit it monthly — the buyer sees the full price with no surprise charges at delivery.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should I use VAT deferral instead?',
    a: 'When you import full containers as a business to hold in stock or re-export within the EU. Deferral keeps import VAT out of your cash flow until the goods actually enter a member state, which is a working-capital advantage — not a tax saving.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use both IOSS and VAT deferral?',
    a: 'They can coexist for different parts of a business. A company that both sells B2C parcels and holds B2B inventory might use IOSS for the parcels and a deferral licence for the container stock. They are complementary, not competing.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    type: 'FAQPage',
    data: {
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="IOSS vs VAT Deferral for China-to-Europe Imports"
  description="IOSS versus VAT deferral for China-to-Europe imports: what each mechanism does, who it is for, and when to use which."
  canonical={`${SITE_URL}/europe/ioss-vs-vat-deferral/`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Europe', href: '/europe/' },
          { label: 'IOSS vs VAT Deferral' },
        ]}
      />
      <h1>IOSS vs VAT deferral</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Two VAT mechanisms that are constantly confused — one is for e-commerce
        parcels, the other for containers of inventory, and picking the wrong
        one is a common, costly mistake.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <blockquote class="tldr">
      <p>
        <strong>TL;DR:</strong> IOSS simplifies VAT on sub-€150 e-commerce
        parcels sold to consumers. VAT deferral (notably the Netherlands\u2019
        Article 23) delays import VAT on full containers held in stock or
        re-exported. They are different tools for different businesses — not
        substitutes.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Side by side</h2>
    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'dimension', label: 'Dimension' },
        { key: 'ioss', label: 'IOSS' },
        { key: 'deferral', label: 'VAT deferral' },
      ]}
      rows={rows}
      caption="Two mechanisms that serve different import models."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>IOSS: solving the parcel problem</h2>
    <p>
      The Import One-Stop Shop launched in July 2021 when the EU abolished the
      old €22 duty-free threshold. From the first euro, every import owes VAT —
      and for the flood of sub-€150 e-commerce parcels, that threatened to
      create a clearance bottleneck and surprise charges at the buyer's door.
      IOSS fixes both: the seller registers in one member state, collects the
      buyer's VAT at checkout, and remits it monthly through a single
      declaration. The buyer sees the full price up front and the parcel clears
      fast.
    </p>
    <p>
      IOSS is a B2C tool. If you sell parcels to EU consumers, it is the
      mechanism to know. It is not for container freight or B2B inventory.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>VAT deferral: solving the cash-flow problem</h2>
    <p>
      VAT deferral is the opposite end of the scale. When a business imports
      full containers to hold in stock or re-export within the EU, the import
      VAT is a large, recurring cash outflow that arrives long before the goods
      are sold. A deferral licence — most famously the Netherlands\u2019 Article 23
      — lets the importer place goods in bonded storage and account for import
      VAT only when they actually enter a member state. It is a cash-flow
      advantage, not a tax saving: the VAT is still owed, just later, when the
      goods move and the revenue exists to cover it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why the Netherlands is the natural home for EU distribution
      operations — the deferral turns a working-capital drain into a
      working-capital asset, and no other member state offers the mechanism as
      cleanly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The common mistake</h2>
    <p>
      The error most first-time importers make is applying the wrong tool to
      the wrong model — registering for IOSS when they actually hold container
      inventory, or expecting VAT deferral to simplify their e-commerce
      parcels. The two mechanisms do not overlap, and neither replaces the
      other. Match the tool to the import model: parcels to consumers, IOSS;
      containers of stock, deferral. If you run both models, you may well need
      both.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <FaqBlock items={faqs} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <Cta />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
